" [:a {:href "/event_names/321"} "Event type"]] [:h1 {:class "event_title"} "Rings, Men" [:small {}]] [:table {:class "biodata"} [:tbody {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Date"] [:td {} "22 – 26 October 1968"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Status"] [:td {} "Olympic"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Location"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/venues/493"} "Auditorio Nacional, Ciudad de México"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/70315"} "117 from 28 countries"]]] [:tr {:class "referees", :style ""} [:th {} "Referee"] [:td {:style "width: 400px"} [:a {:href "/athletes/31332"} "George Gulack"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/USA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/USA.png"}] "USA"]]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "The format was the same as in 1964, with the best six gymnasts in the individual all-around advancing to the apparatus final, with the final apparatus score made up of 50% of the all-around total and a final optional program. The top three qualifiers, in order, were Japan’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/30593"} "Sawao Kato"] ", the all-around gold medalist; his teammate, " [:a {:href "/athletes/30608"} "Akinori Nakayama"] ", the 1966 World Champion; and Soviet " [:a {:href "/athletes/31275"} "Mikhail Voronin"] ", silver medalist at the 1966 Worlds. But Nakayama posted a 9.70 in the final round to win the gold medal. He would win again at the 1970 World Championships and the 1972 Olympics, when he became the second Olympic gymnast to repeat on rings, after Soviet Armenian " [:a {:href "/athletes/31237"} "Albert Azaryan"] " in 1956-60. Voronin won the silver medal in Mexico City, as he would in 1972 at München. Kato struggled in the final round and dropped back to the bronze medal."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "Points"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td